"""crova — the unified content-addressed store.

One store; REAPI, the truss depot, and git are APIs onto it. See docs/design.md
for the thesis, and docs/performance.md for the numbers that constrain it.

This module is CONSUMED BY OTHER MODULES (roma, truss), which drives two choices
below that would be over-engineering in a leaf repo:

  * the crate_universe repo is NAMESPACED (`crova_crates`), not `crates`;
  * every `register_toolchains` is `dev_dependency = True`.

Both exist so that depending on crova imposes nothing on the consumer beyond the
store itself.

NO PROTO TOOLING HERE, DELIBERATELY. The CAS wire contract lives in
//proto/crova/cas/v1, but its generated Rust is CHECKED IN
(crates/crova-proto/src/crova.cas.v1.rs) and regenerated out-of-band by
`cargo run -p protogen`, the same way roma handles roma.build.v1. So the Bazel
target is a plain rust_library and this module needs no rules_proto, no
protobuf, and no rules_rust_prost.

That is worth more than the lines it saves. @rules_rust_prost's DEFAULT
toolchain wires tonic — and through it tokio — into every rust_prost_library.
crova's I/O is async and does use tokio, which is exactly why it must arrive as
an ordinary Cargo dependency the consumer's resolver unifies, never as a second
copy behind a codegen toolchain. Two tokio crates in one process means two
reactor thread-locals, and a future polled on the wrong one panics with `there
is no reactor running` while the pod still reports healthy. Having no codegen
toolchain at all removes that failure mode by construction rather than by
configuring around it.
"""

module(
    name = "crova",
    version = "0.0.5",
)

# ⭐ TLA+ MODEL CHECKING for the mapped index.
#
# The index answers `contains` AUTHORITATIVELY — it skips the syscall — so the property
# that matters is not "is it fast" but "can it ever say ABSENT about a blob that is
# present". That failure is silent and does not self-heal: the client is told the blob is
# missing, and no retry changes the answer. It is also an INTERLEAVING failure (crash
# mid-write, lookup during swap-in), which is exactly what tests are worst at and TLC is
# best at.
#
# ⚠ TLC needs a JDK; it comes from Bazel's built-in java runtime toolchain, so this adds no
# language dependency. rules_tla runs TLC inside a build action, which is why the checks
# appear as build_tests. Same wiring as tomato-bazel/infra//tla.
#
# ⛔ dev_dependency, for the SAME reason register_toolchains is below. A non-dev bazel_dep
# propagates to every transitive consumer: roma would inherit rules_tla — and with it the
# requirement that ITS registry chain can resolve rules_tla — purely for depending on the
# store. Only //tla consumes it, and //tla is only reachable when crova is the ROOT, so a
# dev dep loses nothing: `bazel test //...` here still model-checks. This is the leakage
# the module docstring above claims crova does not impose, and shipping 0.0.5 with a
# non-dev TLA dep would have made that claim false the moment roma depended on it.
bazel_dep(name = "rules_tla", version = "0.1.1", dev_dependency = True)

bazel_dep(name = "rules_rust", version = "0.70.0")

rust = use_extension("@rules_rust//rust:extensions.bzl", "rust")
rust.toolchain(
    edition = "2021",
    versions = ["1.95.0"],
)
use_repo(rust, "rust_toolchains")

# dev_dependency, because a non-dev `register_toolchains` propagates to every
# transitive consumer. Imposing a Rust toolchain on a module that wants the store
# is the leakage tomato-bazel/gate exists to flag, and publishing a module that
# violates the invariant the registry enforces is not a defensible position.
#
# A dev registration still applies when crova is the ROOT, so `bazel test //...`
# here is unaffected. roma and truss register their own — the same call.
register_toolchains(
    "@rust_toolchains//:all",
    dev_dependency = True,
)

# @crova_crates — the crate_universe for //crates/*, resolved from the workspace
# //:Cargo.toml + //:Cargo.lock.
#
# NAMESPACED deliberately. `crate.from_cargo(name = "crates")` is what 14
# registry modules do today and it is a live collision hazard:
# @rules_rust//crate_universe is a SHARED extension, so the repo it generates is
# shared across the module graph. Naming it `crova_crates` means a consumer can
# never collide with us -- and that alone is sufficient, per the note below.
#
# ⛔ NOT `isolate = True`, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS FIX, NOT A SIMPLIFICATION.
# `isolate` is still experimental in Bazel 9, so a module that declares it fails
# to LOAD unless the consumer passes --experimental_isolated_extension_usages:
#
#     Error in use_extension: in call to use_extension(), parameter 'isolate' is
#     experimental and thus unavailable with the current flags.
#
# crova set that flag in its own .bazelrc, which does NOT travel with a published
# module -- a consumer gets MODULE.bazel and nothing else. So every version through
# 0.0.4 was unconsumable without the consumer independently knowing to set an
# experimental flag, and nothing caught it because nothing had ever depended on
# crova. `bazel fetch --repo=@crova` against the registry entry is what surfaced it;
# `bazel mod show_repo` prints the resolved archive and would have stayed green.
#
# Nothing is lost. The comment above is right that the NAMESPACED repo name is the
# real protection: `crova_crates` cannot collide with a consumer's `crates` whether
# the extension is isolated or not. `isolate` was belt and braces, and it turned out
# the belt is what made the trousers unwearable.
crate = use_extension(
    "@rules_rust//crate_universe:extension.bzl",
    "crate",
)
crate.from_cargo(
    name = "crova_crates",
    cargo_lockfile = "//:Cargo.lock",
    manifests = ["//:Cargo.toml"],
)
use_repo(crate, "crova_crates")
